# Pin line endings so a checkout cannot change file CONTENT.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true as a SYSTEM default. Without the rules below, a plain
# `git checkout` on Windows rewrites every text file to CRLF in the working copy -- which made
# the generated CLAUDE.md differ from what `sync-contract.mjs` renders from AGENTS.md by line
# endings alone, producing a permanent phantom `FAIL contract-sync` in `lint-brain.mjs` and in
# the terminal app's Brain Health card. `--write` did not clear it: the next checkout converted
# the file straight back.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the repository AND checks out LF everywhere, so the
# bytes on disk match the bytes in the object store on every platform.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the formats that carry contract, code, or shell semantics. `.md` matters because
# the contract files are Markdown; the hooks matter because a CRLF shebang line makes the kernel
# fail to exec the interpreter on Linux and macOS with a bare "bad interpreter" error.
*.md    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
scripts/git-hooks/* text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one exception -- cmd.exe requires CRLF.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Never touch binaries. Left to `text=auto` a stray CR/LF byte inside one would be "corrected"
# and the file silently corrupted.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.node  binary
*.bundle binary
